# Why queues explode near 100% busy
subtitle: "Run a day at one cashier and watch the wait stay short at half busy, then blow up near 100%, because no slack is left for random bursts."
tags: ["queueing theory","queues","capacity planning","randomness","server utilization","waiting time"]
teaches: ["concepts/queue-dynamics","concepts/server-utilization-nonlinearity","methods/queueing-theory-prediction","concepts/slack-and-buffers"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | a friendly operations engineer who has stood in many queues; relaxed, amused","take":"lines"}

## Why queues explode near 100% busy
use: des_process
with:
    entity: customer
    nodes:
      - {id: arrive, type: source, rate: 0.5}
      - {id: till, type: station, label: cashier, servers: 1, service: 1}
      - {id: leave, type: sink}
    horizon: 480
    seed: 25
    t: 0
    chart:
      - {of: queue, y_max: 30}
      - {of: wait_util, y_max: 25}
sources:
    - id: bagust1999
      title: 'Dynamics of bed use in accommodating emergency admissions: stochastic simulation model'
      authors: Bagust A, Place M, Posnett JW
      year: 1999
      finding: In a simulated hospital, risks of having no bed for an emergency admission rise sharply once average bed occupancy goes above about 85%.
      url: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10406748/

    card title "Why queues explode near 100% busy"

Why does a queue explode when a server gets close to 100% busy?
    draw node_arrive route_arrive_till

Take one cashier: customers arrive at random, one every two minutes on average, and each takes about a minute.
    draw node_till

    draw node_leave route_till_leave

So she is busy half the time. Let's run an eight-hour day.
    draw chart clock reroll

Customers come in clumps, a short line forms now and then, and it soon clears.
    animate t 480 duration 8 easing linear

Over the day, the average wait is under a minute.
    text w50 "50% busy: wait 0.8 min" #2f6b8f x 310 y 445 font_size 54

Now let more customers come, so that she is busy 80% of the time.
    highlight rate_arrive

    erase w50

The same day, re-run: sixty percent more customers, and five times the wait.
    animate nodes.0.rate 0.8 duration 4

    text w80 "80% busy: wait 4.3 min" #2f6b8f x 310 y 445 font_size 54

Queueing theory predicts this curve: the wait grows like busy time divided by idle time.
    draw chart_2

    erase w80

At 95% busy, idle time is a twentieth, and the predicted wait is nineteen minutes.
    animate nodes.0.rate 0.95 duration 5

    text w95 "95%: theory 19 min" #b5482e x 310 y 445 font_size 54

    erase w95

This day came out at ten: near full, one day's average swings wildly around the prediction.
    text w95b "this day: 10 min" #b5482e x 310 y 445 font_size 54

    highlight sim_dot_2

Why? Customers arrive in random bursts, and a line only shrinks while she serves faster than they come.
    highlight chart_line

At half busy she has spare time to catch up; at 95% she has almost none, so every burst lingers.
    point at.ref queue_till gesture circle

    erase w95b

If customers came exactly on schedule and each took exactly a minute, nobody would wait at all: randomness makes the queue.
    text sched "on schedule: no wait" x 310 y 445 font_size 54

    erase sched

That is why hospital planners aim for beds about 85% full, not 100%.
    highlight theory_curve_2

Try it: drag the arrival rate or the service time, or tap new run for another day.
    explore

Near full, waits don't grow in step with busyness; they blow up, because no slack is left to absorb the randomness.

    quiz "A server goes from 90% to 95% busy. Roughly what happens to the average wait?"
        right: It roughly doubles: busy over idle goes from 9 to 19, because idle time halves.
        wrong: Think of busy time divided by idle time.
        * It rises about 5%
        + It roughly doubles
        * It stays the same
---
